Subject: renderer: the level's blocks and edges drawn from one buffer
 (XMOTO_GL_BLOCKS)

Each convex polygon of a block had two buffer objects (positions, texture coordinates) and
was drawn alone (two binds, two pointers, a glDrawArrays of GL_POLYGON), and so was each
polygon of each edge: 954 draws a frame in the credits replay.

LevelGeoms::buildBuffer puts all of a level's block polygons (as the triangles Mesa draws
for GL_POLYGON) and edge quads into one interleaved buffer object once, each block followed
by its edges in the order they are drawn; a block or an edge geom is then one range of it,
and adjacent ranges with the same texture and colour are one draw (xmgl::drawStatic). The
buffers per polygon are not made then. The credits replay draws 18 ranges a frame in place
of 954 draws, for 40 GL draws a frame in all.

On llvmpipe the pixels are the same as Debian's with a draw per range; with adjacent edge
ranges merged, up to 5 pixels a frame differ by 1 in one channel (where Mesa splits the
quads of a longer draw).

XMOTO_GL_BLOCKS=0 draws polygon by polygon again; it needs XMOTO_GL_BATCH (off with it).

